ID: B3-Ch03b-Complicate
TEXT:

"Do not worry, your highness," Albert says, bowing his head. "I will have a talk with him later and straighten this out. I assure you it will not happen again."

"Very well," the king says, waving a dismissive hand. "You are all dismissed. Send word if anything of substance develops. I will remain here in my throne room for the remainder of the day."

"Duly noted," Daren says. "Come on. Back to the waiting room."

"Well," Hadrik mutters the second the heavy doors swing shut behind us. "That was... quite the talk."

"Indeed," Daren says, leading the pace through the stone hallway. "Things are about to get complicated from here on out. That much is certain."

"Think they might try to get rid of us?" Hadrik asks.

"Hardly," Daren murmurs, keeping his voice low as he glances back to make sure Peter and Albert stay out of earshot. "Not while they suspect we are in league with Eiden. They would have to be mad to make an enemy of him over nothing. Still, let us hold the rest until we reach the waiting room. I dislike checking over my shoulder every three paces for ears in the masonry."

We reach the waiting room, hold our tongues until Peter and Albert turn down an adjoining hall, and gather in the far corner. Daren keeps his tone pitched low, beneath the room's ambient chatter.

"Now then," Daren says. "These developments complicate our standing considerably. What is the consensus? Do we maintain our cooperation with the king, or do we cut ties and go our own way?"

"Hard to say," Hadrik grunts, crossing his arms. "The intelligence these rebels pull from their listening devices is far too useful to throw away."

"Agreed," Daren says. "The question is whether they will continue sharing it with us. My inclination is to keep this alliance intact until we see whether we are still getting anything out of it. Kate, what do you think?"

"Do what you want," Kate says, already turning on her heel toward one of the side exits. "I am going back to training. Melindra, let us go."

"Right behind you," Melindra says, falling into step beside her.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Declared, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Declared)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Declared
TEXT:

"Hey, Kate, do not be like that!" Daren calls out. "Come on!"

Kate offers no response, marching ahead with Melindra at her shoulder until the two vanish into the shadows of an outgoing corridor.

"Right," Daren sighs, running a hand over his face. "I take it Kate is not keen on cooperating with the underground king."

"Hardly surprising," Illuna says, her voice flat and cool. "She made her complete distrust of the man abundantly clear only minutes ago. She was never going to celebrate an extended partnership."

"I was not suggesting we become lifelong companions," Daren says defensively. "Only that we hold the arrangement long enough to see if it still serves our purposes."

"Give her some room," Hadrik says. "She might see reason once the air clears."

"Perhaps," Daren mutters. "Very well. Let us return to our tasks and regroup shortly. Arraka, are you ready for another round of aura study?"

"Whatever," Arraka's voice drifts from the amulet. "Not as if my social calendar is brimming with better offers."

"Barry, there you are!" Doctor Reidzek's voice cuts through the room as the others disperse.

Leila's father hurries over, holding my stat booster in his palm. He halts before Leila and me, extending the device outward.

"I have finished my work on your stat booster," he says as I take the metal casing and inspect the dark screen. "However, the internal circuitry requires time to calibrate to the modifications. For the moment, the device is inactive. It should restore itself automatically before long. If it remains unresponsive by tomorrow morning, bring it back and I will examine it."

choice(""Did you manage to unlock all of the aura stats?"", B3-Ch03b-Functional, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Functional, v_b3_ch3_functional = 0)
choice(""How long should it normally take until it starts working again?"", B3-Ch03b-Functional, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Functional, v_b3_ch3_functional = 1)
choice(""Are there any unwanted side-effects that I should be worrying about?"", B3-Ch03b-Functional, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Functional, v_b3_ch3_functional = 2)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Functional
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch3_unexpected,1) if (v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2)
achievement("An unexpected bonus",v_ac_b3_ch3_unexpected)
#if(v_b3_ch3_functional == 0) {
"Did you manage to unlock all of the aura stats?"

"Yes. Magical Sense, Bluff, and Aura Hardening will all be accessible once the device reboots," Reidzek says. "I should also explain that the hardware is configured to wipe your allocated stat points whenever the hidden aura parameters are unlocked. The moment the screen lights up, your stats will sit at zero, and your entire pool of points will be available for redistribution."

"That is incredible," I say. "Does that mean I can reset my point distribution whenever I want by locking and unlocking the aura stats again?"

"I strongly advise against attempting that," Reidzek says, his tone grave. "These units are ancient, and a full point cycle places immense strain on the internal core. That is precisely why there is no standard function to retract points once spent. The reset occurred solely because the aura unlock forced a structural reload. Purposely triggering it again risks corrupting your point allocation permanently, or frying the device beyond repair. Take the clean slate as a rare windfall, and allocate your points with care going forward. You will not receive another reset."

"Understood," I say. "Anything else I should keep in mind?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_functional == 1) {
"How long should it normally take until it starts working again?"

"A few hours at most," Reidzek says. "You will know it has finished when the core emits a sustained tone. I should also explain that the hardware is configured to wipe your allocated stat points whenever the hidden aura parameters are unlocked. The moment the screen lights up, your stats will sit at zero, and your entire pool of points will be available for redistribution."

"That is incredible," I say. "Does that mean I can reset my point distribution whenever I want by locking and unlocking the aura stats again?"

"I strongly advise against attempting that," Reidzek says, his tone grave. "These units are ancient, and a full point cycle places immense strain on the internal core. That is precisely why there is no standard function to retract points once spent. The reset occurred solely because the aura unlock forced a structural reload. Purposely triggering it again risks corrupting your point allocation permanently, or frying the device beyond repair. Take the clean slate as a rare windfall, and allocate your points with care going forward. You will not receive another reset."

"Understood," I say. "Anything else I should keep in mind?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_functional == 2) {
"Are there any unwanted side-effects that I should be worrying about?"

"None of a negative nature," Reidzek says. "Though there is a consequence you will likely welcome. The hardware is configured to wipe your allocated stat points whenever the hidden aura parameters are unlocked. The moment the screen lights up, your stats will sit at zero, and your entire pool of points will be available for redistribution."

"That is incredible," I say. "Does that mean I can reset my point distribution whenever I want by locking and unlocking the aura stats again?"

"I strongly advise against attempting that," Reidzek says, his tone grave. "These units are ancient, and a full point cycle places immense strain on the internal core. That is precisely why there is no standard function to retract points once spent. The reset occurred solely because the aura unlock forced a structural reload. Purposely triggering it again risks corrupting your point allocation permanently, or frying the device beyond repair. Take the clean slate as a rare windfall, and allocate your points with care going forward. You will not receive another reset."

"Understood," I say. "Anything else I should keep in mind?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2) {
"Yes, one final detail," Reidzek says. "When I lifted the lockdown protocol on the casing, the conversion cycle that processes ambient mana into stat points underwent a full reset. As a result, you will receive one of your upcoming stat points early. That means your subsequent allotment will arrive with one fewer point, but having the reserve immediately should serve you far better. If my calculations hold, that extra point will register the second the device activates."

"Good to know," I say, slipping the cool metal casing into my pocket. "Thank you for the help, Doctor."

"Think nothing of it," Reidzek replies with a warm nod. "Now, while we are on the subject... Leila, would you mind returning to the workshop with me for a moment? There is a final calibration check I neglected to run on your device, and I would rather finish it while the benches are clear."

Leila shapes glowing blue letters in the air: "Of course. See you soon, Barry."

"Take care, both of you," I say as they head back toward the medical wing.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2) {
"Nothing of consequence," Reidzek says. "I have shared all the data at my disposal. The application of your device rests entirely in your hands."

"Good to know," I say, slipping the cool metal casing into my pocket. "Thank you for the help, Doctor."

"Think nothing of it," Reidzek replies with a warm nod. "Now, while we are on the subject... Leila, would you mind returning to the workshop with me for a moment? There is a final calibration check I neglected to run on your device, and I would rather finish it while the benches are clear."

Leila shapes glowing blue letters in the air: "Of course. See you soon, Barry."

"Take care, both of you," I say as they head back toward the medical wing.

}
Scanning the chamber to decide my next move, my gaze settles on the archway Kate and Melindra chose. Something about it nags at me. It is not the passage they used for their drills before the king summoned us. Why change locations to an unfamiliar stretch of tunnel?

A layout from earlier falls into place. That particular branch connects directly to the eastern gallery feeding the teleporter chamber. If they are heading there...

I break into a jog, taking the central corridor. The primary passage cuts a straight line toward the teleporter, half the distance of their winding side route. I push through the archway, keeping my footsteps light against the damp stone. If my timing holds, they should be just ahead.

A flash of green cloth appears near the far bend. Kate's cloak. I slow to a silent glide, tucking close to the wall to keep pace unseen.

Melindra halts mid-stride and turns toward the shadows behind her.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Inconvenience, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Inconvenience)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Inconvenience
TEXT:

"Barry, what are you doing?" Melindra calls out, her voice bouncing down the stone passage.

My instincts take over and I duck toward a side alcove, only to stop as the sheer pointlessness of hiding from a stillwater settles in.

"Barry, I felt your aura the instant you set foot in this corridor," Melindra says as I step back into the main tunnel. "What made you think sneaking up on us was going to work?"

"Not my finest tactical move," I admit, closing the distance between us. "Still, finding you here is no coincidence. Where are you two going?"

"Where does it look like?" Melindra says. "If you had the wits to track us this far, the destination should be fairly obvious."

"I know the destination," I say, stopping in front of them. "What I do not understand is why. If you wanted to reach the Overseer before the king's scouts do, why slip off alone? Why not bring the rest of us in on it?"

"Simple enough," Melindra says. "Kate did not want to be a burden. She is already convinced she single-handedly shattered the alliance with the underground king, and she refused to weigh everyone down by announcing a solo excursion to the surface. Besides, who was she going to recruit? You have a dead stat booster, Leila just got her father back, Flower and Illuna are busy feuding, Daren is neck-deep in plague research, and Hadrik has been sulking all afternoon. You know how Kate operates. She never asks for help if she thinks it inconveniences someone."

"She did not seem to mind inconveniencing you," I point out.

"Of course not!" Melindra beams, tossing her ponytail. "When a friendship is solid, convenience stops being a factor. Isn't that the entire point of having friends? Being able to impose on each other without fearing they will hate you for it?"

"You know, Melindra," Kate says, crossing her arms, "when you promised to handle Barry because you knew how his mind worked, this was not what I envisioned."

"It wasn't?" Melindra asks, blinking with wide, innocent eyes. "Why not?"

"Because you were supposed to send him back to the waiting room," Kate says flatly. "Not hand him our entire itinerary."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Honest, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Honest)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Honest
TEXT:

"Do not be difficult, icey," Melindra says. "You know perfectly well you wanted him along."

"I did not," Kate says.

"Of course you did," Melindra counters. "If you truly wanted to lose him, we could have slipped into any side tunnel the second I sensed his aura. You would have left him wandering the dark for an hour. But you never even suggested ducking out of sight. Why do you suppose that is?"

"I simply... didn't think of it," Kate mutters, looking away.

"Really?" Melindra's grin widens. "Is that why your eyes flicked toward every single side passage we passed after I told you he was behind us? Because you weren't thinking about it?"

"Shut up," Kate says.

"A little honesty never hurt anyone," Melindra teases. "Here, repeat after me: 'I wanted Barry to come with us to the surface.' Go on. You have the strength."

"I am going to strike you," Kate says.

"Is it just my imagination, or are you two getting along better than usual?" I ask.

Kate arches an eyebrow. "What is that supposed to mean? When have we not gotten along?"

"Our first day on the road, for starters," I say.

"That was ages ago," Melindra dismisses with a wave. "We haven't had a proper argument since we brought down Talmak together. Have we, icey?"

"No," Kate admits. "We have not."

"Glad we cleared that up," Melindra says. "Now that the team is assembled, let us go find the Overseer. I know the city's layout well enough to locate one of those green-eyed golem heads without running into patrols."

"Neither of you has any intention of turning back, do you?" I ask.

"None whatsoever," Melindra says.

"Fine," I say. "How do we persuade the man at the teleporter to send us up?"

Kate steps directly into my path, her jaw set. "You are not coming, Barry."

"Why not?"

"Your stat booster is offline," Kate says. "You have no defenses."

"Technically, the booster is right here," I say, patting the pocket where the casing rests. "It is recalibrating. It will be functional in a couple of hours. Even without it, I have survived worse. I am Barry the reckless, remember?"

"I thought you were working on tempering that recklessness," Kate says.

"And you were supposed to be working on trusting your companions," I shoot back. "We will call it even."

Glowing blue letters materialize in the air between us: "Do you guys have room for one more, or are you done taking extra passengers?"

We turn to find Leila walking up behind us, a faint smile on her lips.

choice(""Sure you can come! The more, the merrier!"", B3-Ch03b-Merrier, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Merrier)
choice(""Wait, how did you know where to find us?"", B3-Ch03b-Find, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Find)
choice(""Have you been listening to us this whole time?"", B3-Ch03b-Listening, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Listening)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Merrier
TEXT:

"Sure you can come! The more, the merrier!"

"Technically, this is an infiltration, so a smaller group is preferable," Melindra says. "That said, Leila knows how to move without making noise. Welcome aboard."

Leila traces glowing blue letters: "Glad to be of service!"

"I take it you already know what we are doing?" I ask.

"Figured it out the moment I sensed Kate and Melindra heading down this passage," Leila writes. "There are very few reasons to take the surface route right now."

"I am sorry, Leila," Kate says softly. "I did not mean to pull you away from your father so soon after your reunion."

"Do not worry about that," Leila writes back. "You did not force me into anything. I chose to come. Besides, it is only a quick errand, right? We return the moment we finish speaking with the Overseer."

"That is the goal," Melindra says. "Though before we proceed, there is one more detail to address. Breynan, drop the cloak and step out. Your aura suppression is nowhere near refined enough to slip past me."

"I was afraid of that," Breynan's voice drifts from fifty paces down the tunnel as his camouflage dispels, revealing him against the rough stone wall.

Kate's eyes narrow. "What are you doing here? Did the king of the underground send you to spy on us?"

"No," Breynan says, holding up his hands. "It wasn't the king!"

"Then who?" Kate demands.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Status, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Status)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Find
TEXT:

"Wait, how did you know where to find us?"

"I caught a glimpse of you heading into this tunnel in a hurry while I was following my father towards his workshop, so I figured that something must have gone wrong," Leila writes. "I wasn't sure if I should go after you at first, but I eventually made up my mind and told my father that we'd need to schedule my stat booster's maintenance for some other time, so that I could catch up to you in time."

"I take it you already know what we are doing?" I ask.

"Figured it out the moment I sensed Kate and Melindra heading down this passage," Leila writes. "There are very few reasons to take the surface route right now."

"I am sorry, Leila," Kate says softly. "I did not mean to pull you away from your father so soon after your reunion."

"Do not worry about that," Leila writes back. "You did not force me into anything. I chose to come. Besides, it is only a quick errand, right? We return the moment we finish speaking with the Overseer."

"That is the goal," Melindra says. "Though before we proceed, there is one more detail to address. Breynan, drop the cloak and step out. Your aura suppression is nowhere near refined enough to slip past me."

"I was afraid of that," Breynan's voice drifts from fifty paces down the tunnel as his camouflage dispels, revealing him against the rough stone wall.

Kate's eyes narrow. "What are you doing here? Did the king of the underground send you to spy on us?"

"No," Breynan says, holding up his hands. "It wasn't the king!"

"Then who?" Kate demands.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Status, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Status)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Listening
TEXT:

"Have you been listening to us this whole time?"

"Not the entire time, but I caught the last minute or so," Leila writes.

"I take it you already know what we are doing?" I ask.

"Figured it out the moment I sensed Kate and Melindra heading down this passage," Leila writes. "There are very few reasons to take the surface route right now."

"I am sorry, Leila," Kate says softly. "I did not mean to pull you away from your father so soon after your reunion."

"Do not worry about that," Leila writes back. "You did not force me into anything. I chose to come. Besides, it is only a quick errand, right? We return the moment we finish speaking with the Overseer."

"That is the goal," Melindra says. "Though before we proceed, there is one more detail to address. Breynan, drop the cloak and step out. Your aura suppression is nowhere near refined enough to slip past me."

"I was afraid of that," Breynan's voice drifts from fifty paces down the tunnel as his camouflage dispels, revealing him against the rough stone wall.

Kate's eyes narrow. "What are you doing here? Did the king of the underground send you to spy on us?"

"No," Breynan says, holding up his hands. "It wasn't the king!"

"Then who?" Kate demands.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Status, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Status)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Status
TEXT:

"I came on my own initiative," Breynan says, stepping closer. "I followed you because I want to join the excursion. You intend to contact the Overseer, correct? I have had more dealings with that construct than anyone else in the network. I understand how its logic operates. If you take me along, I can locate the green-eyed spheres far faster, and I can give the teleporter operator a plausible pretext so he sends us up without asking questions. My only condition is that I speak to the Overseer first."

"And what prevents you from reporting our entire discussion to the king the moment we return?" Kate asks, her arms remaining folded.

"I have no intention of telling the king anything," Breynan says firmly. "I realize how we appear to outsiders, but not every rebel here is content to grovel. Some of us retain our dignity. I will not let the king treat me like Peter and Albert, bowing to every whim. He knew how vital it was for me to attend these negotiations, yet he barred me purely to remind everyone who holds authority. If that is his posture, I have no obligation to follow his every directive. I act on my own judgment, even if it jeopardizes my standing underground."

"His aura is steady," Melindra says, tilting her head. "No ripples of deceit."

"I agree," Leila writes. "And if he is telling the truth, running to the king afterward would be handing over proof of his own insubordination."

Kate lets out a long breath.

"Breynan," she says, "I cannot claim to trust you, but having you beside us is safer than leaving you behind with a motive to betray us. If no one objects, let us move before another patrol happens upon this tunnel."

choice(""Shouldn't we at least let Daren know where we're going?"", B3-Ch03b-Daren, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Daren, v_b3_ch3_daren = 1)
choice(""Alright, let's move."", B3-Ch03b-Teleporter, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Teleporter, v_b3_ch3_daren = 0)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Daren
TEXT:

"Shouldn't we at least let Daren know where we're going?"

"That would be wise," Leila writes. "If complications arise and we are delayed, someone should know where to look."

"If we contact Daren, we must ensure no one in his vicinity overhears," Kate says. "A stray word to the king ruins everything."

"Leave it to me," I say, drawing the transceiver from my pocket and turning the dial to Daren's frequency.

"Daren, can you hear me?" I whisper into the brass mesh.

"Yes, Barry," Daren's voice answers. "What is happening?"

"Drop the receiver volume to minimum and hold it tight to your ear," I say.

"Right... give me a moment."

A pause stretches over the line before his voice returns, faint and muffled.

"Ready. What is it?"

"Keep your reactions quiet, and stick to vague answers so you give nothing away," I say. "Are you listening?"

"Yes, yes, I am listening. Go on."

"Kate, Melindra, Leila, and I are heading to the surface to meet the Overseer on our own," I say. "Behind the king's back. Breynan is coming with us."

"WHAT?!" Daren roars, the volume rattling the casing.

"Daren," I hiss. "What did I just say about reactions?"

"Right—sorry," he mutters, lowering his tone to a strained whisper. "Why would you do something so reckless? We agreed to deliberate until morning!"

"Kate was about to go alone with Melindra," I say. "She felt responsible for the broken alliance and decided to take the initiative. She will not turn back."

"Unbelievable," Daren grumbles. "Hold your position. I am coming with you."

"Stay where you are and focus on the plague," I say. "You have less than a week before the infection becomes contagious. Do not abandon your research unless there is an emergency. We can take care of ourselves."

A heavy sigh crackles through the receiver. "Fine. But give me your word you will take no foolish risks. If you encounter an ambush, request an immediate extraction. Do you hear me?"

"Understood," I say. "We will be careful."

"Call me the instant you return," Daren says.

"Will do. Closing the channel now."

I tap the release catch and slide the transceiver back into my pocket.

"Looks like we are clear," Melindra says. "Let us move."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-TeleporterB, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-TeleporterB)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Teleporter
TEXT:

"Alright, let's move."

"Breynan, there is one detail I failed to ask earlier," Kate says as we make our way down the corridor toward the staging room. "If you intended to bypass the king and seek out the Overseer alone, why join us? Why not go by yourself?"

"Solo excursions draw scrutiny," Breynan says. "If I requested an unscheduled trip to the surface alone, the guards would have grown suspicious. Despite the blade on my hip, frontline combat is not my specialty. I rely on illusions, with some light healing when required. My cover story is that I am taking our newest allies above to walk the guard routes, ensuring we coordinate smoothly on future deployments."

"Think the operator will buy that?" I ask.

"Barry? He will not question it," Breynan says. "Let me do the talking and everything will proceed smoothly."

"Let us hope so," I say.

We reach the staging room, where Barry the teleporter operator stands behind the brass-and-seredium console governing the massive mechanism behind him. He spots our group, offering a casual wave before turning to Breynan.

"Afternoon, Breynan," the operator says. "Taking the new recruits out for their first assignment?"

"A training walk, primarily," Breynan replies smoothly. "Walking them through the standard patrol routes so they know how to navigate the districts if a team splits. The last thing we need is another accidental skirmish with the royal guard."

"No combat on the agenda, then," the operator says.

"None," Breynan says. "A brief orientation run. We will be back well before dinner."

"Keep in mind, if trouble finds you anyway, I cannot pull you back until you break line of sight and calm your auras," the operator cautions, tapping the dial. "We cannot afford another disaster like Desmond."

"We know the risks, Barry," Breynan says. "We will be cautious."

"And make sure you reach the designated drop zone before signaling on the transceiver!" the operator adds. "The emergency override puts tremendous strain on the core. It is not an everyday shortcut."

"Understood," Breynan says. "Send us to the primary coordinates."

"Coordinates locked," the operator says, throwing a pair of heavy levers. "Safe travels."

White light blazes across our vision, the hum of the platform rising to a teeth-rattling vibration as space folds around us. When the glare subsides, the stale cavern air gives way to the chill breeze of the surface. We stand in a narrow, trash-strewn alleyway tucked into the city's lower tier. Above the rooftops, the sky burns in bruised shades of rust and copper, the sun dipping close to the horizon.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Desmond, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Desmond)


ID: B3-Ch03b-TeleporterB
TEXT:

"Breynan, there is one detail I failed to ask earlier," Kate says as we make our way down the corridor toward the staging room. "If you intended to bypass the king and seek out the Overseer alone, why join us? Why not go by yourself?"

