ID: B3-Ch03b-Vulnerability
TEXT:

Leila draws glowing blue letters in the air: "What about the king? You keep mentioning the security of the palace, but never that of the king. Is the king in a more vulnerable position now than he was before?"

The sphere hovers in place, offering no response to the text.

"He cannot read your script, Leila," I say. "Overseer! Leila asks whether the king of Ollendor is in a more vulnerable position now than he was before."

"Affirmative," the Overseer replies. "The king is indeed in a more vulnerable position than before, as a result of Eiden's arrival in the city."

"Explain," Breynan says.

"King Undriel previously spent the vast majority of his days in the palace," the Overseer states. "Since Eiden's arrival, his majesty has initiated personal inspections across crucial municipal facilities to ensure operations meet exacting standards. The king fears Eiden will depart if the slightest imperfection is observed in Ollendor, driving him to conduct frequent public tours. This compromises his security, as the royal guard cannot maintain the protective perimeter available within the throne room. Provided his schedule and transit routes are known in advance, the probability of a successful assassination increases substantially."

"Can you provide that schedule?" Breynan asks.

"Negative," the Overseer says. "That information is classified."

"Classified, is it?" Breynan mutters. "Is there unclassified information regarding the king's movements you can disclose? Any tactical detail useful for planning an interception during transit?"

#if(v_b3_ch2_name == 0) {
"The king deploys advance scouts along scheduled routes to sweep for hazards," the Overseer says. "If you can identify who these scouts are, you could easily figure out which route king Undriel is planning to take next. The scouts are typically sent one day in advance, and then they are sent again a few hours before the actual trip, to see if anything has changed in the meantime. Knowing this information, you should be able to make reasonably accurate predictions of when the king and his guards would be leaving the palace. Another strategy that could be used is to simply wait for the king to visit one of the facilities, and then try to ambush him on his way back, but it is not advisable, because the king's inspections are usually very short, and there would be no time to set up any proper traps that wouldn't immediately be neutralized by the royal guards."

"Now that is genuine intelligence," Breynan says. "Many thanks!"

"Happy to be of assistance, Breynan," the Overseer replies. "Would the group named 'Felran, the strongest fire elementalist on the Eastern Continent' like to ask a question of me as well?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch2_name == 1) {
