"Listen," Breynan says, holding up an open hand. "I don't know who sent you here, but we don't mean to hurt you. Please, get out of the way."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Platform, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Platform)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Interrupt
TEXT:

"Why did you interrupt your conversation with Eiden all of a sudden, earlier today?"

"The Overseer protocol does not recall having interrupted any conversation with Eiden today," the Overseer says. "Could you please be more specific?"

"You know what conversation I'm talking about," I say. "It's the one where Eiden kept questioning you about your true motives. Right at the end, Eiden asked you what you were truly after, and then you cut him off by giving him an automated answer. Why did you stop the discussion there?"

"Question unclear," the Overseer says. "Unfortunately, the Overseer protocol cannot provide you with a correct answer if your question is too vague. Please rephrase your previous query."

"How was that unclear?" I say. "That question was as direct as they come!"

The Overseer says nothing, continuing to hover in silence.

"You're not going to get anything else out of him," Breynan tells me. "He's just going to keep repeating 'Question unclear' until you give up. It's the standard phrase that he always uses when he doesn't want to answer someone's question."

"So that's how it's going to be, then," I say.

"Does anyone else have any questions for the Overseer?" Breynan says.

"Pressing him further is pointless," Kate says. "He has already told us all he knows about the king of Ollendor's vulnerabilities."

"I think so too," Breynan says. "Maybe we should get ready to head back, then. Alright, Overseer, that will be all for today. Have a safe trip."

"Goodbye, Breynan," the Overseer says, and then the metal ball flies into the air once more, in order to continue its patrols.

"Let's go this way," Breynan says, and then he heads back the way we came.

"So, can we know for sure that anything of what the Overseer told us is true?" I say, as we're all moving towards the exit.

"Not yet, but we should be able to easily verify all this information," Breynan says. "As soon as we get back to our base, we can start by—"

Breynan suddenly cuts off as three men dressed in rags step into our path at the alley's mouth, clutching daggers in white-knuckled grips. Two of them tremble with fear, but they refuse to budge.

"Listen," Breynan says, holding up an open hand. "I don't know who sent you here, but we don't mean to hurt you. Please, get out of the way."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Platform, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Platform)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Platform
TEXT:

"We can't!" one of the men cries, his voice cracking with terror. "The man in black robes said if you leave this alley, he will butcher our families! He knows where our children sleep! We can't let you pass!"

"You don't have to die for him," Breynan says, taking a measured step forward with his hands open. "Come with us into the underground tunnels. I am with the resistance. We can shelter you and your kin."

"Stay back!" the man shrieks, slashing his knife through empty air. "Don't come near me! I'm warning you!"

"We do not have time to negotiate," Kate says, sweeping her arm outward.

Thick columns of jagged ice erupt from the flagstones, throwing up a frozen palisade between us and the desperate men.

"What are you doing?" Breynan asks. "You just penned us in."

"I am lifting us over," Kate says, already forming an ice platform beneath our boots. "Brace yourselves."

Before the platform gains an inch of height, the ice splinters and dissolves into white vapor. Kate drops to one knee, clutching her temples with both hands as a strangled gasp tears from her throat.

"Kate, what is it?" I ask, stepping to her side.

"I can't... concentrate," she manages, eyes clamped shut against the pain. "The pressure in my skull... barely able to form words."

"A heavy dizziness hex," Melindra says, her eyes darting across the surrounding rooftops. "Smashed straight through her mental wards. But there are no casters in range. Who channeled it?"

"A lessathi relic," Breynan says, drawing his sword. "Probably a royal guard operative. Not all of them possess innate magic."

"Without an aura, they are indistinguishable from the rest of the city's populace," Melindra says grimly. "We dropped our guard. I am getting us out of here."

She gathers the air currents to lift us, but the lead man in rags charges forward, thrusting his blade at her.

"You're not escaping!" he screams.

Breynan steps into the line, his steel clashing against the dagger and knocking the strike aside. The other two men rush forward, forcing Leila and me to draw our weapons.

"I cannot maintain flight while you are locked in melee!" Melindra shouts as our boots touch down again. "Clear your targets so we can ascend!"

Heavy footsteps echo behind us as three more armed men in rags pour into the far end of the alley, closing the pincer. I cannot afford hesitation.

choice("Aim to incapacitate, without killing anyone.", B3-Ch03b-Glance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Glance, v_b3_ch3_civilians = 0)
choice("Kill only if necessary.", B3-Ch03b-Glance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Glance, v_b3_ch3_civilians = 1)
choice("Kill everyone who attacks me.", B3-Ch03b-Glance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Glance, v_b3_ch3_civilians = 2)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Glance
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch3_civilians == 0) {
Their clumsy footwork gives them away immediately. None of these men has ever held a blade in combat. Even without my stat booster active, basic martial training is more than enough to handle them.

I slip past the lead man's wild slash, drive my dagger into the meat of his thigh, and snap a front kick into his solar plexus to send him crashing onto his back. Whipping the hand crossbow from my harness, I drop the three flankers behind me with rapid bolts to their knees and shins.

Just as the flankers hit the cobblestones, the first man scrambles back to his feet with desperate speed, lunging knife-first at my chest.

I pivot and fire a bolt into his other leg to break his momentum, but his forward weight still carries his blade into my left flank. Steel bites into flesh, and warm blood soaks my tunic.

"Looks like you could use mending," Breynan says, hurrying over after knocking his opponent unconscious with his hilt. Leila already has her target pinned flat.

I press a hand over the wound to stem the bleeding while the man on the ground groans, clutching his punctured legs. Breynan places his palms over my torn side; white light pours between his fingers, soothing the sharp tear of the flesh until the pain dulls to a faint ache.

"Father!" a young voice cries. A boy of ten dashes into the alley, throwing himself down beside the wounded man.

"I'm alright," the father wheezes, pushing the boy back weakly. "Return to your mother. It isn't safe here."

"You're bleeding!" the boy sobs. "We have to find a doctor!"

"We need to move before reinforcements arrive," Breynan says, pulling his hands away as the healing takes hold.

We skirt past the fallen man and the boy, slipping out of the alley mouth into the wider thoroughfare. Ahead, a woman rushes past in tears, heading straight for the alley—the boy's mother.

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_civilians == 1) {
Their clumsy footwork gives them away immediately. None of these men has ever held a blade in combat. Even without my stat booster active, basic martial training is more than enough to handle them.

I slip past the lead man's wild slash, drive my dagger into the meat of his thigh, and snap a front kick into his solar plexus to send him crashing onto his back. Whipping the hand crossbow from my harness, I drop the three flankers behind me with rapid bolts to their legs.

Just as the flankers hit the cobblestones, the first man scrambles back to his feet with desperate speed, lunging knife-first at my chest.

A leg shot will not halt his forward lunge in time. Instinct overrides hesitation. I raise the crossbow and drive a bolt straight into the center of his chest.

The man drops his dagger, clawing feebly at the wooden shaft in his ribs as the strength drains from his limbs. He collapses hard against the stones, convulsing once before going entirely still. Around me, Breynan and Leila finish subduing their attackers and regroup.

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_civilians == 2) {
Their clumsy footwork gives them away immediately. None of these men has ever held a blade in combat. Even without my stat booster active, basic martial training is more than enough to handle them.

I slip past the lead man's wild slash, drive my dagger deep into his gut, and kick him sprawling across the stones. Whipping the hand crossbow from my harness, I put three quick bolts through the foreheads of the flankers behind me, dropping them in their tracks.

The man I gutted scrambles back up with feral frenzy, driving his knife straight at my throat.

I bring the crossbow up in one fluid motion and fire a bolt into his chest. The man drops his blade, tearing at the quarrel buried in his breast before his legs give out. He hits the pavement flat, shuddering until life leaves his eyes. Around me, Breynan and Leila finish subduing their attackers and regroup.

}
#if(v_b3_ch3_civilians == 1 || v_b3_ch3_civilians == 2) {
"We need to get out of here before anyone else tries to attack us," Breynan says. "I don't want any more innocent lives to be lost today--"

"Father?" a young voice calls from the alley entrance.

A boy of ten spots the corpse at my feet and sprints forward, dropping to his knees on the bloody stones.

"No..." the boy whispers, staring into the unseeing eyes. "No, no, no, you cannot be dead. You promised everything would be fine. You promised we would go home together. Wake up... please, tell me you are only sleeping. Please..."

He clutches his father's bloodstained tunic with small, white-knuckled fists, trembling violently.

"Let's go," Breynan tells us. "There is nothing else we can do here."

"You..." the boy's voice hardens as we turn to leave. "You did this..."

I look back. The boy's tear-streaked face locks onto mine, his gaze burning with raw hatred as his eyes drop to the crossbow still in my grip, its tip wet with his father's blood.

"I will never forgive you," the boy says, his voice rising to a ragged scream. "I will never forgive you!"

"This way," Breynan says, urging me forward.

I turn away, following him into the street. Outside the alley, a woman stands with her hands pressed over her face, weeping silently—the dead man's wife. We pass her without a word, heading for the extraction point.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Uniforms, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Uniforms)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Uniforms
TEXT:

"We have to find a quiet stretch to settle our auras so we can trigger the recall," Breynan says, leading us briskly down a wide, deserted street lined with shadowed alleyways. "For now, put distance between us and that ambush."

"We still haven't found the one who hexed Kate," Melindra says. "As long as that relic is active, we are exposed."

"We don't need total safety," Breynan says. "We only need enough calm for the teleporter core to lock on."

"I could carry us on the wind," Melindra suggests.

"No aerial movement," Breynan warns. "The patrol spheres will engage the moment you take flight. Their directives prioritize airborne targets above all else."

"We can't afford a pitched battle either," Melindra counters. "Kate cannot weave spells, my oath prevents me from harming locals, and Barry is fighting without a stat booster. Do you expect you and Leila to hold off the royal guard alone?"

"Once we clear this street, there won't be a need for fighting," Breynan says. "We just have to reach the far end—"

"Look out!" Melindra shouts, yanking Breynan backward by his tunic.

A roaring sheet of flame erupts from a side alley, engulfing the stones where he stood a fraction of a second prior. Heat washes over our faces from the towering wall of fire.

"You missed," a voice calls from the alley behind us. "How did you miss? Couldn't you wait one more second before triggering the charge?"

"Not my fault," another voice snarls from behind the flames. "The girl pulled him back before he crossed my sights. Doesn't matter. The fire has them pinned."

"I told you both that engaging without backup was foolish," says a third voice from the slanted roof of a nearby building. "We are only trainees. We should have waited for the guard captain."

"That is why you will remain a trainee, Derek," the first man sneers. "You never make full guard by waiting for the captain to clean up your mess."

Three men emerge in the distinctive cobalt-blue coats of the royal guard, marked with minimal insignia. Each wields a weapon fitted with an elemental gemstone: the man behind us holds a trident with a brown topaz, the one on the roof carries a sword with a diamond in its hilt, and the man beside the flames wears a gauntlet studded with a ruby. Lessathi-forged channeling artifacts.

"None of them are mages," Melindra whispers. "I sensed their auras earlier, but I didn't realize they were recruits using relic weapons... Kate, can you fight?"

"I... don't know," Kate says, pressing a palm against her pounding forehead. "I will have to."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Summoner, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Summoner)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Summoner
TEXT:

"I didn't spend two years mastering this relic just to stand watch in the dirt," the man with the trident sneers. "Today, I prove my worth to the high guard, and you two are backing me up!"

"As if we have an alternative," Derek mutters from the rooftop. "Let us finish this."

"Brace yourselves!" Breynan warns.

Kate moves first, sending a volley of razor-sharp icicles at the trainee behind us. The man plunges his trident into the pavement; a thick slab of stone thrusts upward from the flagstones, catching the volley and shattering the ice before he yanks the weapon free, letting the rock crumble.

Above, Derek ascends on swirling air currents channeled through his diamond blade, while the recruit in the alley levels his ruby gauntlet and unleashes a roaring beam of fire straight at our cluster.

Kate throws up an ice wall to intercept the flames, but the barrier dissolves the instant the blast clears as she clutches her temple, trembling from the dizziness hex. Breynan slams the butt of his staff into the ground. A flash of blinding white radiance blankets the street, and from the brilliance emerge three massive, winged manticores—beasts of phantom light and muscle that vault into the air, each diving toward a separate target.

"A summoner!" the man with the trident curses, backing away as the first manticore bears down on him.

"Bring them down!" Derek yells in a panic, wheeling awkwardly in mid-air. "I cannot outmaneuver a manticore in flight!"

"Drop to the ground and fight it, then!" the trident trainee snaps, hurling stone projectiles at the beast diving for his throat. "My hands are full!"

The gauntlet trainee fires wild bursts of flame toward the beast circling his rooftop, every blast passing harmlessly through open air.

Leila traces glowing blue letters: "I will take one down while they are occupied."

She breaks into a sprint toward the man with the trident.

"Leila, stop!" Melindra shouts, but Leila is already closing the distance.

Just as she reaches striking range to plunge her blade into his back, the trainee pivots with a cruel grin and jams his trident deep into the cobblestones. A massive stone fist punches out of the earth beneath Leila, slamming into her ribs with bone-crushing force. The impact launches her through the air; she hits the flagstones hard, her head striking stone before she goes completely limp.

"Did you truly think I couldn't recognize an illusion?" the man sneers, pulling the trident from the ground as the stone fist fractures into gravel. "I was waiting for one of you to step into range of my stone fist. Say farewell to your friend."

He steps toward Leila's motionless form, only to crash headlong into an invisible wall of pressurized air.

"What in the hells...?" He pats the transparent barrier, searching for an edge.

Warm wind suddenly coils around my arms and legs.

"Bring her back here!" Melindra commands, pointing toward Leila's unconscious body. "Quickly! I cannot maintain this wind barrier for long."

choice("Fly towards Leila and carry her back to our group.", B3-Ch03b-Carry, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Carry, v_b3_ch3_carry = 1)
choice("Fly towards the man with the trident, and try to kill him.", B3-Ch03b-Trident, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Trident, v_b3_ch3_carry = 0)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Trident
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch3_job,1) if (v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 1)
set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 1)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 0)
achievement("A job well done!",v_ac_b3_ch3_job)
"I'm on it!" I say, letting Melindra's air current propel me high into the air to clear the wind barrier and line up a shot on the trident trainee.

I level my hand crossbow and fire a volley downward. The recruit anticipates the angle, dodging sideways and driving his trident into the ground to summon several heavy boulders from thin air. With a sweep of his weapon, he flings them upward at terrifying velocity.

I bank hard on the wind currents, slipping past the flying stones, and snap two more bolts back at him. He doesn't bother moving, raising a quick earthen barrier to deflect the quarrels.

"Stop trading shots with him and secure Leila!" Melindra shouts from below. "The drones are tracking you! Look up!"

Six red-eyed metallic spheres dive out of the cloud cover, homing in on my elevation. I snap off shots from a distance, but the drones bank aside with effortless precision, closing the gap. From below, the trainee capitalizes on the distraction, hurling a relentless barrage of boulders to box me in.

#if(v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 1) {
While dodging the barrage in mid-air and fumbling for another bolt, an agonizing pulse of pressure tears through my skull. The world spins violently, nausea rising in my throat as my senses blur into static. I drop my weapon, clutching my head with both hands in a futile attempt to steady the vertigo.

The dizziness hex.

Before I can reorient my flight, a jagged boulder rips through the blind spot, striking my temple with crushing force. Bone gives way, and the dark takes me.

}
#if(v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 0) {
While dodging the barrage in mid-air, a dull throb pulses in my temples accompanied by a momentary wave of nausea. The dizziness hex strikes, but the protective ward Illuna layered into my aura a week ago absorbs the brunt of the assault, keeping the disorientation manageable.

Ignoring the lingering ache, I bank through the incoming boulders, steady my aim, and take down two of the descending drones with precise quarrels through their sensory lenses. The remaining four close to lethal range, opening up with alternating beams of fire and frost that force me into evasive rolls.

Four sharp icicles streak up from the street below, punching clean through the metal casings of the remaining drones and sending them crashing to the pavement. Kate signals frantically from the ground, urging me down.

I dive toward the street, scoop Leila into my arms, and carry her behind Melindra's wind barrier. Behind us, the trident trainee screams at his companions in fury.

"What are you two doing?!" he roars. "Why are you still fighting phantoms? They aren't real! Stop dodging and let them hit you!"

"As if I will stake my throat on your intuition!" Derek shouts back from above, swinging his sword to send wind gusts at the manticore pursuing him. "Prove it!"

"Have you never seen a manticore?" the trainee yells. "Look at the one I fought! The moment I called the bluff, it stopped diving and started circling aimlessly! And why would the automated drones ignore massive flying beasts unless the protocols flagged them as illusions?"

"Fine," the gauntlet trainee mutters, lowering his weapon just as a manticore dives at his chest.

The moment he stops resisting, the beast flares its wings and banks smoothly back into the sky, joining the other phantoms in an aimless circle.

}
choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart) if (v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 1)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load) if (v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 1)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 1)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Nonsense, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Nonsense) if (v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 0)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Carry
TEXT:

"I'm on it!" I say, letting Melindra's air currents glide me low over the flagstones toward Leila.

The man with the trident spots my advance and hurls a spread of stone shards, but they shatter harmlessly against Melindra's wind barrier. I slide to a stop beside Leila and gather her into my arms. The trainee drives his trident into the earth to conjure a stone fist, but the spell erupts ten paces short, punching empty air.

With Leila secure, I glide back to our perimeter while the trainee abandons the assault to vent his frustration at the others.

"What are you two doing?!" he roars. "Why are you still fighting phantoms? They aren't real! Stop dodging and let them hit you!"

"As if I will stake my throat on your intuition!" Derek shouts back from above, swinging his sword to send wind gusts at the manticore pursuing him. "Prove it!"

"Have you never seen a manticore?" the trainee yells. "Look at the one I fought! The moment I called the bluff, it stopped diving and started circling aimlessly! And why would the automated drones ignore massive flying beasts unless the protocols flagged them as illusions?"

"Fine," the gauntlet trainee mutters, lowering his weapon just as a manticore dives at his chest.

The moment he stops resisting, the beast flares its wings and banks smoothly back into the sky, joining the other phantoms in an aimless circle.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Nonsense, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Nonsense)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Nonsense
TEXT:

"Well, look at that," Derek says, lowering his blade as the third manticore banks away into the clouds. "These rebels made fools of us. Time to return the favor."

"Wonderful," Melindra mutters, raising a wide, shimmering dome of compressed air over our group. "Kate, how is the headache? Any chance of an ice dome?"

"I couldn't hold it for a second in this state," Kate says, pressing both hands against her temples. "I'm sorry."

"Had to ask," Melindra says.

A split second later, the barrier shudders under a relentless barrage. Fire streams, rock shards, and scything blades of wind pound the perimeter from three sides. The air hums violently under the strain, and within ten seconds, beads of sweat trace down Melindra's jawline.

"Melindra, that's enough," Kate urges, reaching for her shoulder. "If you force the matrix any longer, you risk losing control and triggering the enchanter's oath. This is my fault. Leave me and fly out with Leila. You and Barry can still escape."

"Nonsense," Melindra grits through clenched teeth. "I am only reinforcing ambient air. There is no resonance cycle to backfire, even if the dome fails."

She tilts her head upward and projects her voice over the roar of the assault.

"Still, it would be magnificent if SOMEONE finished weaving their spell! How much longer do you intend to make me hold this?!"

"Who is she talking to?" Derek calls out from the rooftop.

"Who cares?" the trident trainee scoffs. "A desperate bluff. If they had reinforcements nearby, we would have seen—"

The sky darkens.

A deafening roar tears through the air, like a tidal wave crashing through a canyon.

Above the rooftops, the atmosphere splits apart. Three colossal serpents of pressurized water—each stretching over a hundred feet in length and six feet thick, their liquid jaws gaping wide—plunge out of the sky in terrifying unison.

The impact is breathtaking. The raw mass of the water blocks out the sun, crushing downward before anyone can draw breath.

"Gods above—" the trident trainee chokes out. "Retreat! Run!"

Derek screams, throwing all his wind magic into flight, but the lead serpent crashes over him like a collapsing mountain, swallowing him whole and sweeping him across the rooflines. The other two serpents strike in the same instant, scooping the remaining recruits from the street in a churning torrent and carrying them far into the distance before crashing into the lower quarter.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Interpersonal, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Interpersonal)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Interpersonal
TEXT:

"Was that... Illuna's water magic?" Kate asks, staring up at the empty sky where the torrents just cleared the street.

"It was," Illuna's voice drifts from a side alley as she steps into the light, letting her aura suppression drop.

Her eyes gleam vivid blue, her expression cold and composed. Beneath the surface, Flower's aura is quiet and faint, deep in sleep.

"I thought you were never going to finish weaving that spell!" Melindra says, resting her hands on her hips. "You just had to shape three full serpents, didn't you?"

"One for each target," Illuna says in a dry, even cadence. "A surprise strike of that volume yields only one opening. You did not wish them to escape."

"Fine, fine," Melindra says. "Point taken."

"I don't understand," Kate says, blinking. "Illuna, how did you find us?"

"I tracked your group from the moment you entered the tunnels," Illuna states clinically. "Melindra detected my aura early on and chose not to disclose my presence. When you took the platform to the surface, I had the operator send me up on the next cycle. I followed because I knew you would find trouble."

"And find it we did!" Melindra says cheerfully.

Illuna turns her sharp gaze toward Kate. "Why didn't you come to me before leaving the base?"

"I..." Kate hesitates, looking down. "I didn't want to be a bother."

Illuna shakes her head, her brow furrowing in quiet exasperation.

"I thought we were already past this phase of our interpersonal interaction," Illuna says flatly. "Please tell me we are not still at the stage where we communicate through small talk."

"No," Kate says softly, meeting her eyes. "You're right. We are not."

"Good," Illuna says. "I detest small talk."

"I hate to break up the reunion," Melindra cuts in, looking down the avenue. "I sense the royal guard captain, that mercenary, and several other mages closing on our position. They are moving fast. Breynan, can we trigger the extraction?"

"We still need a moment to settle, but I will get Barry on the line," Breynan says, pulling out his transceiver.

"Were those three the only hostiles?" Illuna asks.

"There is another operative who hit Kate with a dizziness hex," I say. "We haven't located him."

"He is likely stationed inside one of the surrounding tenements," Melindra says. "The buildings are full of civilians, and without an aura to track, there is no way to single him out."

choice(""As long as he can only cast dizziness spells, we shouldn't need to worry about him."", B3-Ch03b-Worry, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Worry)
choice(""Do you think we should go and look for him?"", B3-Ch03b-Look, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Look)
choice(""At least we know where he'll be coming from, if he tries to attack us."", B3-Ch03b-Attack, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Attack)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Worry
TEXT:

"As long as he can only cast dizziness spells, we shouldn't need to worry about him."

"We don't know for certain that dizziness is his only capability," Melindra says.

"If he carried lethal relics, wouldn't he have joined the assault earlier?" I ask.

"The trainees said their initial plan was to wait for the royal guard before engaging," Melindra points out. "The relic user may be holding his hand for the same reason."

"Come on, Barry, pull us out!" Breynan barks into his transceiver.

"Your auras are too turbulent!" the operator's panicked voice buzzes over the line. "You need to calm down! And... oh gods, is one of you unconscious?"

"We can't be calmer than this!" Breynan shouts back. "The royal guard is breathing down our necks. Teleport us out now!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Headache, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Headache) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Transceiver, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Transceiver) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Look
TEXT:

"Do you think we should go and look for him?"

"No, we don't have time for a search," Melindra says. "The royal guard will be on us within minutes. We need to clear out immediately."

"I just hope he doesn't strike the moment we trigger the platform," I say.

"If he steps into the open, I will drop a wind barrier on him before he reaches us," Melindra says. "We will spot him the second he leaves cover."

"Come on, Barry, pull us out!" Breynan barks into his transceiver.

"Your auras are too turbulent!" the operator's panicked voice buzzes over the line. "You need to calm down! And... oh gods, is one of you unconscious?"

"We can't be calmer than this!" Breynan shouts back. "The royal guard is breathing down our necks. Teleport us out now!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Headache, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Headache) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Transceiver, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Transceiver) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Attack
TEXT:

"At least we know where he'll be coming from, if he tries to attack us."

"Exactly," Melindra says. "The moment anyone steps out of those doors with hostile intent, I will seal them behind a wind barrier."

"How close is the guard detachment?" I ask.

"They are closing fast," Melindra says. "With luck, we will be gone before they round the corner."

"Come on, Barry, pull us out!" Breynan barks into his transceiver.

"Your auras are too turbulent!" the operator's panicked voice buzzes over the line. "You need to calm down! And... oh gods, is one of you unconscious?"

"We can't be calmer than this!" Breynan shouts back. "The royal guard is breathing down our necks. Teleport us out now!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Headache, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Headache) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch03b-Transceiver, v_current_scene = B3-Ch03b-Transceiver) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Headache
TEXT:

"You know what happened to Desmond!" the operator cries through the speaker. "I can't risk it!"

"We are dead if you don't pull us out!" Breynan barks back. "This is no time to worry about a mild headache!"

"You know full well it is far worse than a headache!" the operator shouts over the static. "What if the core shears off a limb? Are you willing to gamble with that?"

"You're exaggerating!" Breynan snaps. "We don't have time to debate—"

The air behind Illuna implodes with a sharp crack.

A figure in black robes materializes from nothingness, driving a narrow dagger straight toward her spine. Illuna twists at the final fraction of a second, deflecting the blade from her heart, but the steel punches deep through her chest.

"No!" Kate screams, throwing a volley of jagged icicles that forces the assassin to leap back. "Get away from her!"

The assassin wrenches the dagger free. Illuna collapses onto the cobblestones, blood spreading beneath her ribs as her eyelids flutter shut.

I catch the glint of multiple rings on the killer's fingers, each set with a different gemstone—lessathi relics. With a flick of his wrist, a spectral blade forms from thin air, whistling toward Kate's throat.

"Breynan, mend her!" Kate shouts, knocking the conjured blade aside with an ice shield as she sways against the dizziness.

"What is happening over there?!" the operator yells through the transceiver as Breynan throws himself to the ground beside Illuna, white healing light pouring from his hands into the wound.

"Brace yourselves!" Melindra shouts, her eyes widening in alarm. "Gontrok is inbound at terrifying speed! He will be on us in seconds!"

"Did you hear that, Barry?" Breynan roars into the brass receiver. "Trigger the recall now!"

"The safety limits... I cannot—"

"Damn the limits!" Breynan bellows. "I take full responsibility! Pull the lever!"

"Gontrok is almost here!" Melindra screams.

"Fine!" the operator shrieks. "Don't blame me for the landing!"

Blinding white radiance envelops our vision. The platform frequency rises to a deafening, bone-rattling hum. Through the glare, I catch a single glimpse of Gontrok tearing around the corner at impossible speed, his face twisted in fury as he roars, "Damn it!"

Then reality tears apart.

Transit hits like having every muscle, tendon, and organ shredded simultaneously. The agony is blinding, overwhelming my senses until the world goes black.

When my vision clears, I am on my hands and knees against cold rock, heaving violently until my stomach has nothing left to give.

I wipe my mouth, struggling to draw breath. The air is damp and metallic. We are in a vast underground cavern bounded by dark seredium strata, but there is no extraction platform, no control console, and no rebel sentries. Ahead, Breynan kneels over Illuna, desperately pouring white light into the hole in her chest.

"This..." I rasp, leaning against the rock face. "This isn't anywhere near the rebel base, is it?"

choice("Next chapter", B3-Ch04a-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: B3-Ch03b-Transceiver
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch3_unforeseen,1)
achievement("An unforeseen premonition",v_ac_b3_ch3_unforeseen)
"Barry!" Rose's voice bursts through the transceiver in my pocket, sharp and panicked. "Answer me quickly! Where are you? Is Illuna with you? Are you on a street lined with crooked buildings?"

"Yes," I say, pressing the brass casing through my tunic. "Yes to both, but how do you—"

"Illuna, look out!" Rose screams over the channel. "I just saw a premonition! Someone is about to teleport behind you! Move, now!"

"A premonition?" I blurt out. "Since when can you—"

The air behind Illuna implodes with a sharp crack as a figure in black robes materializes from nothingness, driving a dagger toward her heart. Warned by Rose's shout, Illuna springs sideways; the blade misses her chest, opening a shallow cut along her waist as she rolls across the stones.

"No!" Kate screams, launching a barrage of ice cones that forces the assassin to leap backward. "Stay away from her!"

The killer lands lightly, rings gleaming on every finger with multi-colored gemstones—lessathi relics.

"Illuna, watch his hands!" I shout. "Those rings channel—"

"I see them," Illuna says in a cold, steady tone, snapping a whip of pressurized water toward his legs.

The assassin teleports out of the path, manifests a conjured blade from thin air, and flings it straight at her head.

"Brace yourselves!" Melindra shouts, glancing down the avenue. "Gontrok is inbound at terrifying speed! He will be on us in seconds!"

"Did you hear that, Barry?" Breynan roars into his transceiver. "Trigger the recall now!"

"The safety limits... I cannot—"

"Damn the limits!" Breynan bellows. "I take full responsibility! Pull the lever!"

"Gontrok is almost here!" Melindra screams.

"Fine!" the operator shrieks. "Don't blame me for the landing!"

Blinding white radiance envelops our vision. The platform frequency rises to a deafening, bone-rattling hum. Through the glare, I catch a single glimpse of Gontrok tearing around the corner at impossible speed, his face twisted in fury as he roars, "Damn it!"

Then reality tears apart.

Transit hits like having every muscle, tendon, and organ shredded simultaneously. The agony is blinding, overwhelming my senses until the world goes black.

When my vision clears, I am on my hands and knees against cold rock, heaving violently until my stomach has nothing left to give.

I wipe my mouth, struggling to draw breath. The air is damp and metallic. We are in a vast underground cavern bounded by dark seredium strata, but there is no extraction platform, no control console, and no rebel sentries. The others slowly push themselves up from the ground, equally disoriented.

"This..." I rasp, leaning against the rock face. "This isn't anywhere near the rebel base, is it?"

choice("Next chapter", B3-Ch04a-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = B3-Ch04a-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


